WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Model artifacts often move through many stages before they reach production or shared use.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:19.720
A checkpoint may start in research, then be fine-tuned, converted, quantized, merged, renamed, packaged with tokenizer files, stored in a registry, cached in a deployment environment, and copied into automation workflows.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.240
Each movement creates a trust boundary.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:34.820
Metadata can be lost, files can be mixed with the wrong companion artifacts, assumptions can become stale, and a model that was acceptable for experimentation can quietly become part of a business workflow.

5
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.960
The risk is not only external artifacts.

6
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:43.660
Internal does not automatically mean understood, current, or approved.

7
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:55.620
A reliable lifecycle records source, version, transformation steps, evaluation status, approval status, storage location, deployment context, and retirement state.

8
00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:07.800
Those records should be visible where engineers actually make decisions: repositories, model registries, storage paths, deployment pipelines, release notes, and operational documentation.

9
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.760
Trust boundaries should also reflect who is responsible.

10
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:14.820
Research teams may validate exploration results.

11
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.600
Platform teams may control packaging and registry promotion.

12
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:24.460
Security and governance teams may review license, provenance, and intake evidence.

13
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.680
Operations teams may own rollout, monitoring, rollback, and retirement.

14
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.080
The goal is traceability without slowing every experiment.

15
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:40.220
Experimental artifacts can move quickly inside controlled spaces, but shared and production artifacts need clear status.

16
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:49.280
A model lifecycle is dependable when people can tell whether a checkpoint is experimental, approved, superseded, revoked, or retired.
